From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Johannes Waigel <johannes@waigel.me>
Subject: [PATCH] Check every member's constraints, not just the first

SEQUENCE_constraint and SET_constraint walk a type's members and validate
each in turn, but both leave the loop on the first iteration.

In SEQUENCE_constraint the branch taken when a member has no member-level
constraint returns the result of the member type's own constraint instead
of keeping it and continuing:

	if(elm->encoding_constraints.general_constraints) {
		int ret = elm->...general_constraints(...);
		if(ret) return ret;              /* correct */
	} else {
		return elm->type->...general_constraints(...);   /* ends the loop */
	}

So validation stops at the first member that carries no constraint of its
own. SET_constraint has the same shape in both branches, so it never checks
past member zero.

The effect is silent: asn_check_constraints() reports success for a value
that violates a constraint the compiler did emit a checker for. With the
GSMA SAIP profile schema,

    ProfileHeader ::= SEQUENCE {
        major-version UInt8,                    -- no member constraint
        minor-version UInt8,
        profileType   UTF8String (SIZE (1..100)) OPTIONAL,
        iccid         OCTET STRING (SIZE (10)),  -- memb_iccid_constraint_1
        ...

major-version is first and has no member-level constraint, so the loop
returns there and memb_iccid_constraint_1 is never reached: a profile whose
ICCID is four octets rather than ten passes asn_check_constraints() without
a word. Verified against four GSMA test profiles (120 ProfileElements):
unchanged before the fix, and after it the short ICCID is reported while
the untouched profiles stay clean.

CHOICE_constraint returning after one member is correct -- a CHOICE has
exactly one alternative present -- and SET_OF_constraint already loops
properly. Only these two are affected.

The bug dates from a5972bed ("Combined constraints and introduced value
randomizer"), which rewrote the member-constraint lookup and kept the
early return that the old single-branch form had.
---
--- a/skeletons/constr_SEQUENCE.c
+++ b/skeletons/constr_SEQUENCE.c
@@ -1062,8 +1062,9 @@
 				ctfailcb, app_key);
 			if(ret) return ret;
 		} else {
-			return elm->type->encoding_constraints.general_constraints(elm->type,
+			int ret = elm->type->encoding_constraints.general_constraints(elm->type,
 				memb_ptr, ctfailcb, app_key);
+			if(ret) return ret;
 		}
 	}
 
--- a/skeletons/constr_SET.c
+++ b/skeletons/constr_SET.c
@@ -1015,11 +1015,13 @@
 		}
 
 		if(elm->encoding_constraints.general_constraints) {
-			return elm->encoding_constraints.general_constraints(
+			int ret = elm->encoding_constraints.general_constraints(
 					elm->type, memb_ptr, ctfailcb, app_key);
-		} else {
-			return elm->type->encoding_constraints.general_constraints(
+			if(ret) return ret;
+		} else {
+			int ret = elm->type->encoding_constraints.general_constraints(
 					elm->type, memb_ptr, ctfailcb, app_key);
+			if(ret) return ret;
 		}
 	}
 
